      "id": "rdls_exp-ken_rcmrd_housingmaterialbrick",
      "title": "Housing Material_Brick",
      "description": "Housing materials are part of the Population and Housing indicators in DHS, used to assess household living standards, wealth index composition, and environmental health conditions.The DHS records materials for floors, walls, and roofs. Bricks can either fall under category two if unburnt or catgory three if burnt. Rudimentary Materials These represent an intermediate level - locally made or partially processed materials, often transitional between poor and durable housing. Floor: Wood planks, bamboo, unfinished concrete Wall: Unburnt bricks, wood planks, poles with mud Roof: Wood, sticks, rustic materials Reflects modest or semi-permanent structures; common in peri-urban settings. Finished (Improved) Materials These materials are durable, processed, and offer protection from the elements. Floor: Cement, polished wood, vinyl, tiles, marble, carpet Wall: Burnt bricks, cement blocks, concrete, stone Roof: Iron sheets, tiles, concrete, asbestos sheets Denotes improved housing quality, characteristic of higher wealth quintiles. [Source: This metadata record was automatically extracted from the GeoNode portal rcmrd]",
